2026-09-03T08:43Z Second review round on PR #255. Both pause clauses now run a positive control first: the same change is made with the target running, and a change creating no work leaves the clause not-run rather than green — without it the finding 'a counter did not move' is produced by any target whose change would not have moved one. install runs first where offered. The stubs' pause gate moved from _edit_one_item to _bump: three fixtures override the former, so recomputes-whole-library-on-edit and duplicates-work-on-company were going red on R22, fail-controls failing clauses they are not about. drive() now records a verb that raised but re-raises when every verb raised, since check_no_egress grades returncode 0 plus zero traced attempts and would have read 'exercised nothing' as green.
2026-09-03T08:55Z Third review round on PR #255, five findings, three structural. (1) The restart clause read its baseline across the restart, so any work a target does on start landed inside the graded window — a red on a target whose pause held. The window now opens after the restart settles, which is what the clause's own wording says. (2) The positive control ran BEFORE the graded phase and made the same change it would then make again: on a target where the second edit is a no-op the graded phase finds no work for reasons unrelated to the pause, a false green. It now runs AFTER, via resume, so a consumed change becomes not-run instead of pass — costing both clauses a dependency on resume, which the contract calls the other transition of the same control. (3) assess() now records every assertion through one guard: guarding call sites inside assertions did not converge, since settle() reads status at five more points and perturb catches only NotImplementedError. An assertion that raises reaches the artifact as not-run and the run completes. Also: --drive exits DRIVE_INCOMPLETE when a verb raised inside it, and check_no_egress reads that as not-run — zero attempts from a path that never executed is the same number as zero attempts from a path that stayed home.
2026-09-03T09:08Z Fourth review round on PR #255, five findings. The decisive one: the resume-based positive control from round 3 made both clauses permanently not-run for every real target in the tree — zoteus offers pause and declares resume absent by a documented ruling, so no target could ever be decided. The control is now a SECOND target instance in an arena of its own, never stopped: nothing is consumed (it is not the graded target) and nothing is asked of resume. It must not share a root, the opposite of R13's pair, and a shared root is refused rather than measured. Also: check_no_egress no longer downgrades to not-run when the tracer actually recorded an attempt (under a no-route namespace the egressing verb is usually the one that then raises, so testing the return code alone would have downgraded the exact target the clause exists to catch); drive() no longer re-raises when every verb raised, since that exit was graded as a red; assess()'s guard re-raises UnsupportedVerb, which interface.py documents as the loud signal that an assertion forgot Declaration.offers; and the prose and artifact fields left stale by round 3's reorder are corrected.
2026-09-03T09:19Z Fifth review round on PR #255, four findings, all fixed. A target whose process never starts raises out of running() before the verb loop, so --drive now guards the lifecycle too and exits DRIVE_INCOMPLETE rather than 1, which the egress clause grades as a red. The control's independence guard now reads what an adapter argued is NOT derived state as well as its derived-state roots: that is where a target's source library is declared and where the change is actually made, so two instances sharing one library would have the control consume the graded change — the same false pass by the other route, and one no stub can reach since their edit is arena-local. A declaration naming no path at all cannot demonstrate independence and is refused. The incomplete-run not-run now carries subject and control in its detail, since run_fixtures reads detail['subject'] for this check and a measured zero must not publish as null.
2026-09-03T09:34Z Sixth review round on PR #255: eight findings, none about the logic — the reviewer states R22 admits no false pass or false red as implemented. Fixed: the control's own failures are now reported as the control's rather than published as false statements about the graded target; the independence guard uses containment rather than exact path equality, so a nested control root is refused; the restart block re-reads whether counters exist rather than inheriting the answer from before the restart; a perturbation that cannot be driven is recorded against status, as durability.py records the same failure, rather than against the pause verb that worked; the DRIVE_INCOMPLETE exit contract now has a test through main() for both its guards, which could previously be deleted with the suite green; and three prose claims that had gone stale across the rounds. Left as it stands with a reason: the committed acceptance-fixtures.json carries no R22 row, and regenerating it here would publish R10-no-egress as not-run for want of an isolation mechanism — an artifact measured where half the instrument is missing is worse than one that is visibly older. README now discloses that, and also that a target the harness cannot hand a separate library reaches not-run on both clauses for a real reason.
2026-09-03T11:42Z DO NOT SWAP v1.12.0 FOR v1.13.0 IN THE ENTRIES ABOVE, and do not write 'latest' either. Three reasons, the third being the one that decides it. Every v1.12.0 in this ticket sits in a log entry, and the log is append-only: the 2026-08-31 note was true when written, and editing it would make the record lie about what was known then. Second, that note's instruction carries a measured justification -- the search layer moved +1738/-57 over ten files, index-manager.ts alone +454 -- so swapping the version would keep the arithmetic and point it at a different diff, a sentence that looks verified and is not. Third and decisive: the version is not what changed, the scope is. Upstream released v1.13.0 on 2026-09-03 and issue #48 is in it, verified in source rather than from the maintainer's summary -- sqlite-index.ts prepares SELECT ... FROM passages WHERE vector IS NULL ORDER BY pid LIMIT ?, build.ts's notice says action:build resumes and re-fetches nothing, and StartBuildOptions documents that action:build deliberately does not set fresh. So the embed-phase resume slice this ticket was carrying is BUILT, as #24's and #34's were before it, and status now reports passagesWithoutVectors. What remains is freshness, counters, pause, serve-stale, custody and query semantics minus whatever else that release moved. THE MEASURED DELTA: v1.12.0..v1.13.0 is 30 commits over 67 files, +4912/-524, with sqlite-index.ts +458/-28 and index-manager.ts +286/-27 -- nearly three times the move that already justified 'do not re-file from the v1.10.0 reading'. THE STANDING FORM, replacing a hardcoded version: re-derive against the reviewed baseline named in UPSTREAM, and re-baseline first when make upstream-status reports movement. That is version-free and still pinned, where 'latest' is version-free and unpinnable -- a reader cannot tell whether the last re-derivation was against the then-latest, which is the judgement-outliving-its-subject failure UPSTREAM exists to prevent.
2026-09-03T12:38Z claude note THE EMBED-PHASE RESUME SLICE IS BUILT, and this ticket's remaining scope shrinks by one of its six named parts. Upstream #48 merged in v1.13.0 (`28367ea`) and the baseline now names it (ticket 0618). What he built is what the 2026-09-02 comment from here specified: `sqlite-index.ts` prepares SELECT id, item_key, title, text, source FROM passages WHERE vector IS NULL ORDER BY pid LIMIT ?, read by a new passagesMissingVectors(limit); a build whose embedder gave up KEEPS its checkpoint instead of clearing it and WITHHOLDS the library version stamp, so a later action:update cannot run a ?since delta over a half-embedded index and falls back to the resuming build; and backfillVectors() pages 500 at a time, runs only on a resume, and breaks if a round fails to reduce the shortfall. Retry is five attempts, exponential from a second, Retry-After replacing rather than adding to the exponential term, on 429/408/5xx and network errors and deliberately not on 400/401/403. So: of scoped issue A's six parts -- ledger, freshness, counters, pause, serve-stale, custody and query semantics -- the RESUME half of freshness is now upstream, as #24's crawl-progress half and #34's migration-and-salvage half were before it. THREE of this ticket's slices have now been built by the maintainer from third-party reports this repository scoped. What remains is the counters (still absent: metrics.ts is untouched by the release and carries a handful of names, none per stage per trigger per outcome), the PAUSE (still absent: zotero_index's action list is unchanged at build|refresh|update|status|stop), serve-stale, custody and query semantics. The tool-surface enumeration in the 2026-09-02 note is re-verified at v1.13.0 and unchanged. Re-derive the rest against b0e0bc8 before arguing it, as the 2026-08-31 note already says -- the search layer moved another +1830/-198 over fifteen files with query-terms.ts new.
